Managing a professional DevOps pipeline requires a clear separation between how code moves (Git branching) and how it is deployed (Jenkins/Argo CD).

1. Branching Strategy (Dev, QA, UAT, Prod)
To segregate environments,  should use GitFlow or Environment Branching.

Feature Branches: Create these from develop. Once the task is done, merge back to develop.

Develop Branch (Dev): The integration branch. Every commit here triggers a build/deploy to the Dev Environment.

QA/Release Branch: When code is stable in Dev, merge develop into a release/* branch. This triggers deployment to the QA Environment for testing.

UAT/Staging Branch: After QA passes, merge into a staging branch for User Acceptance Testing (UAT).

Main Branch (Prod): After UAT approval, merge to main (or master). This is the "Gold" version. Tags like v1.2.0 (Sigma/Semantic Versioning) are applied here.

Sigma (Semantic) Versioning
Use the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH format (e.g., 1.2.4):

Major: Breaking changes.

Minor: New features (backward compatible).

Patch: Bug fixes.

2. Jenkins Git Configuration & Triggers
Why  build isn't triggering:
If you have 100 releases and use --depth 1, Jenkins only sees the "tip" of the branch.

The Issue: If your webhook or "Poll SCM" relies on comparing the current commit to the previous one, and Jenkins didn't fetch the history, it might fail to detect the change.

Fix: In Jenkins Git settings, ensure "Shallow clone" is unchecked or set the depth high enough (e.g., --depth 5) to allow for merge analysis.

Git Config Tip: Use git config --global pull.rebase true to keep a linear history, making it easier for Jenkins to track changes.

3. Jenkins SSO with AWS (SAML vs. OIDC)
Integrating Jenkins with AWS IAM Identity Center (formerly AWS SSO):

Feature	SAML 2.0	OIDC (OpenID Connect)
Protocol	XML-based	JSON/REST-based
Security	Older, but industry standard for SSO.	More Secure/Modern. Uses JWT tokens and is better for APIs.
Plugin	Jenkins "SAML Plugin"	Jenkins "OIDC Provider Plugin"
Setup Steps:

AWS Side: In IAM Identity Center, create a Customer Managed Application.

Metadata: Download the SAML Metadata XML from AWS.

Jenkins Side: Install the SAML Plugin. Go to Security -> Security Realm -> SAML 2.0. Paste the XML metadata.

Groups: Map AWS Groups (e.g., DevOps-Admin) to Jenkins Roles using the Role-Based Strategy Plugin.

4. Deployment: EKS, EC2, and Fargate
Deployment depends on how much "server management" you want to do:

EC2: Standard Virtual Machines. You manage the OS, patching, and scaling.

EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service): Managed Kubernetes. You manage the "Nodes" (EC2) or use Fargate to run "Serverless" Pods.

Fargate: Serverless compute for containers. You don't manage any servers; you just provide the Docker image and pay for CPU/RAM used.

5. Argo CD vs. Jenkins (The "GitOps" Way)
The most modern approach is to use Jenkins for CI and Argo CD for CD.

Jenkins (Build):

Clones code from Git.

Builds Docker Image.

Scans image with Trivy or Snyk.

Pushes image to AWS ECR.

Crucial Step: Jenkins updates a separate Git repo (the "Manifest Repo") with the new image tag.

Argo CD (Deploy):

Argo CD "watches" the Manifest Repo.

The moment Jenkins updates the tag, Argo CD sees the "Out of Sync" status.

It automatically "Syncs" (pulls) the change and updates the EKS or K8s cluster.

Deployment Strategies
Blue-Green: You have two identical environments (Blue=Old, Green=New). You switch 100% of traffic to Green. If it fails, you switch back to Blue instantly.

Canary: You deploy the new version to only 5% of users. If they don't report bugs, you slowly increase to 100%. Argo Rollouts is the best tool for this on Kubernetes.

Your Docker + Jenkins Pipeline Task
Here is the Jenkinsfile logic to build, scan, and prepare for Argo CD:

Groovy
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('CI: Build & Scan') {
            steps {
                sh 'docker build -t my-app:${BUILD_NUMBER} .'
                // Critical check: Fail build if high vulnerabilities found
                sh 'trivy image --severity CRITICAL --exit-code 1 my-app:${BUILD_NUMBER}'
            }
        }
        stage('CD: Update Manifest') {
            steps {
                // This 'pushes' to the repo ArgoCD is watching
                dir('manifest-repo') {
                    sh "sed -i 's/tag:.*/tag: ${BUILD_NUMBER}/' deployment.yaml"
                    sh "git commit -am 'Update image to ${BUILD_NUMBER}' && git push"
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
